1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,064
Sweetheart, cornbread dressing is the dish that

2
00:00:03,064 --> 00:00:06,195
disappears first from every Christmas table I've

3
00:00:06,195 --> 00:00:06,977
ever sat at.

4
00:00:07,157 --> 00:00:09,408
Golden and crispy on top,

5
00:00:09,408 --> 00:00:11,479
soft and savory inside,

6
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:14,541
full of sage and onion and celery.

7
00:00:14,721 --> 00:00:16,950
But most of the dressing I taste

8
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:18,970
is either dry as sawdust or a

9
00:00:18,970 --> 00:00:20,294
soggy, mushy brick.

10
00:00:20,474 --> 00:00:23,980
And it's always the same five mistakes.

11
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:26,405
Every one of them is easy to

12
00:00:26,405 --> 00:00:26,726
fix.

13
00:00:27,076 --> 00:00:29,643
So tonight I'm giving you the cornbread

14
00:00:29,643 --> 00:00:32,407
dressing my mother made every Christmas on

15
00:00:32,407 --> 00:00:34,053
Elm Street, step by step.

16
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:35,452
And stay with me,

17
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:38,033
because the last secret is one thing

18
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:39,826
she did the night before,

19
00:00:39,826 --> 00:00:41,762
with a plain kitchen towel,

20
00:00:41,762 --> 00:00:43,913
that made hers the best in the

21
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:46,853
whole church, and nobody ever guessed it.

22
00:00:47,203 --> 00:00:50,106
First, honey, let's settle the argument.

23
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:52,887
Stuffing goes inside the bird.

24
00:00:53,067 --> 00:00:55,946
Dressing is baked in its own pan.

25
00:00:56,126 --> 00:00:59,503
Up North, most families say stuffing either

26
00:00:59,503 --> 00:01:01,074
way, and down South,

27
00:01:01,074 --> 00:01:03,352
they say dressing either way,

28
00:01:03,352 --> 00:01:06,259
and they've been arguing about it for

29
00:01:06,259 --> 00:01:07,515
a hundred years.

30
00:01:07,695 --> 00:01:10,489
My mother's people came from Kentucky,

31
00:01:10,489 --> 00:01:12,622
so in our house it was always

32
00:01:12,622 --> 00:01:15,416
dressing, and it was always cornbread.

33
00:01:15,596 --> 00:01:17,221
Never white bread.

34
00:01:17,571 --> 00:01:20,209
Cornbread dressing goes back to the Native

35
00:01:20,209 --> 00:01:21,527
peoples of the South,

36
00:01:21,527 --> 00:01:24,039
who were baking cornmeal long before any

37
00:01:24,039 --> 00:01:25,106
settlers arrived.

38
00:01:25,286 --> 00:01:28,438
Poor Southern families made it because cornmeal

39
00:01:28,438 --> 00:01:30,650
was cheap and wheat flour wasn't.

40
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:33,345
You used yesterday's cornbread,

41
00:01:33,345 --> 00:01:34,724
a few vegetables,

42
00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:36,914
and whatever broth you had.

43
00:01:37,094 --> 00:01:38,762
It was a way of making a

44
00:01:38,762 --> 00:01:40,917
little bit of meat feed a whole

45
00:01:40,917 --> 00:01:42,307
family at Christmas.

46
00:01:42,487 --> 00:01:44,480
In a minute I'll tell you why

47
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,817
the old cornbread matters so much.

48
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:49,731
My mother made a double batch every

49
00:01:49,731 --> 00:01:50,976
Christmas, honey.

50
00:01:51,156 --> 00:01:52,252
One pan for us,

51
00:01:52,252 --> 00:01:54,811
and one pan for the Lutheran church

52
00:01:54,811 --> 00:01:56,566
supper on Christmas Eve.

53
00:01:56,746 --> 00:01:58,240
And every year,

54
00:01:58,240 --> 00:01:58,638
Mrs.

55
00:01:58,818 --> 00:02:01,490
Hartley, who thought she was the best

56
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:02,789
cook in Mansfield,

57
00:02:02,789 --> 00:02:04,666
would take a big spoonful,

58
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:05,749
chew it slowly,

59
00:02:05,749 --> 00:02:06,977
and say, Dorothy,

60
00:02:06,977 --> 00:02:08,060
what's in this?

61
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:11,003
And my mother would just smile and

62
00:02:11,003 --> 00:02:12,628
say, love, Margaret.

63
00:02:12,808 --> 00:02:13,935
Just love.

64
00:02:14,115 --> 00:02:16,355
She never told her about the towel.

65
00:02:16,705 --> 00:02:18,808
I learned to make it standing on

66
00:02:18,808 --> 00:02:20,581
a wooden chair next to her,

67
00:02:20,581 --> 00:02:21,698
when I was seven.

68
00:02:21,878 --> 00:02:24,455
My job was crumbling the cornbread.

69
00:02:24,635 --> 00:02:27,637
Danny's job was tearing the white bread,

70
00:02:27,637 --> 00:02:29,813
and he always ate half of it.

71
00:02:29,993 --> 00:02:31,460
My mother would say,

72
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:33,513
Daniel, if you eat the glue,

73
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:35,566
the whole thing falls apart.

74
00:02:35,746 --> 00:02:38,054
We didn't know what she meant by

75
00:02:38,054 --> 00:02:39,136
glue for years.

76
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:41,841
I'll tell you exactly what she meant

77
00:02:41,841 --> 00:02:42,683
in a minute.

78
00:02:43,032 --> 00:02:45,368
Here's what you need for a nine

79
00:02:45,368 --> 00:02:46,574
by thirteen pan.

80
00:02:46,754 --> 00:02:48,871
One big pan of cornbread,

81
00:02:48,871 --> 00:02:51,072
about eight cups crumbled,

82
00:02:51,072 --> 00:02:52,512
made a day ahead.

83
00:02:52,692 --> 00:02:55,734
Four slices of white sandwich bread,

84
00:02:55,734 --> 00:02:57,255
torn up and dried.

85
00:02:57,435 --> 00:02:59,165
One stick of butter.

86
00:02:59,345 --> 00:03:01,198
Two cups of chopped onion,

87
00:03:01,198 --> 00:03:03,408
and two cups of chopped celery.

88
00:03:03,588 --> 00:03:04,645
Two eggs.

89
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:07,591
About four cups of chicken or turkey

90
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:08,052
broth.

91
00:03:08,232 --> 00:03:11,356
And two tablespoons of fresh sage,

92
00:03:11,356 --> 00:03:13,468
or two teaspoons dried.

93
00:03:13,818 --> 00:03:16,362
Plus a teaspoon of dried thyme,

94
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:18,576
a teaspoon of black pepper,

95
00:03:18,576 --> 00:03:20,053
and salt to taste.

96
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:23,127
Some families add a little cooked sausage

97
00:03:23,127 --> 00:03:24,680
or chopped boiled egg.

98
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:26,467
My mother added neither,

99
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:29,079
because the cornbread and the sage were

100
00:03:29,079 --> 00:03:30,084
the whole show.

101
00:03:30,264 --> 00:03:32,938
And one very important thing,

102
00:03:32,938 --> 00:03:33,492
honey.

103
00:03:33,672 --> 00:03:36,098
The cornbread must not be sweet.

104
00:03:36,278 --> 00:03:38,948
Sweet cornbread makes dessert dressing.

105
00:03:39,128 --> 00:03:41,112
Use buttermilk cornbread,

106
00:03:41,112 --> 00:03:42,223
with no sugar,

107
00:03:42,223 --> 00:03:44,841
or just a tablespoon at the most.

108
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:48,025
And the cornbread itself is simple,

109
00:03:48,025 --> 00:03:48,511
honey.

110
00:03:48,691 --> 00:03:50,896
Two cups of yellow cornmeal,

111
00:03:50,896 --> 00:03:52,234
one cup of flour,

112
00:03:52,234 --> 00:03:54,597
a tablespoon of baking powder,

113
00:03:54,597 --> 00:03:56,093
a teaspoon of salt,

114
00:03:56,093 --> 00:03:58,692
two eggs, two cups of buttermilk,

115
00:03:58,692 --> 00:04:01,369
and a half stick of melted butter.

116
00:04:01,549 --> 00:04:03,778
Heat your cast iron skillet in a

117
00:04:03,778 --> 00:04:06,774
four hundred twenty-five degree oven with a

118
00:04:06,774 --> 00:04:08,515
spoonful of bacon grease,

119
00:04:08,515 --> 00:04:10,744
pour the batter into the hot pan

120
00:04:10,744 --> 00:04:11,719
so it sizzles,

121
00:04:11,719 --> 00:04:14,157
and bake about twenty-five minutes.

122
00:04:14,507 --> 00:04:17,898
Mistake number one is using fresh cornbread.

123
00:04:18,078 --> 00:04:20,833
Fresh cornbread is full of moisture,

124
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:23,283
and when you pour broth over it,

125
00:04:23,283 --> 00:04:25,044
it collapses into mush.

126
00:04:25,224 --> 00:04:28,145
That's why so many people's dressing is

127
00:04:28,145 --> 00:04:29,194
a soggy brick.

128
00:04:29,374 --> 00:04:31,558
You want the cornbread stale,

129
00:04:31,558 --> 00:04:32,010
honey.

130
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:33,920
Bake it the day before.

131
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,410
Crumble it into big pieces.

132
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:38,789
And leave it out on a sheet

133
00:04:38,789 --> 00:04:40,825
pan overnight, uncovered,

134
00:04:40,825 --> 00:04:42,128
so it dries out.

135
00:04:42,308 --> 00:04:45,633
Remember that, because it's connected to the

136
00:04:45,633 --> 00:04:46,162
secret.

137
00:04:46,512 --> 00:04:49,273
If Grandma's recipe is helping you,

138
00:04:49,273 --> 00:04:51,403
tap the like button for me,

139
00:04:51,403 --> 00:04:52,271
sweetheart.

140
00:04:52,451 --> 00:04:55,667
It helps more people find these videos.

141
00:04:55,847 --> 00:04:58,714
Mistake number two is skipping the white

142
00:04:58,714 --> 00:04:59,144
bread.

143
00:04:59,324 --> 00:05:01,669
Cornbread alone falls apart.

144
00:05:01,849 --> 00:05:03,809
A few slices of torn,

145
00:05:03,809 --> 00:05:06,795
dried white bread act like glue.

146
00:05:06,975 --> 00:05:09,180
They hold it together so you can

147
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:10,421
cut a nice square,

148
00:05:10,421 --> 00:05:12,281
instead of scooping crumbs.

149
00:05:12,461 --> 00:05:15,153
My mother always used four slices,

150
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:16,895
the heels of the loaf,

151
00:05:16,895 --> 00:05:19,032
which nobody wanted anyway.

152
00:05:19,382 --> 00:05:22,657
Mistake number three is raw vegetables.

153
00:05:22,837 --> 00:05:25,345
If you throw raw onion and celery

154
00:05:25,345 --> 00:05:26,333
into the pan,

155
00:05:26,333 --> 00:05:28,613
they never soften all the way,

156
00:05:28,613 --> 00:05:31,046
and you get crunchy bits in soft

157
00:05:31,046 --> 00:05:31,730
dressing.

158
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:34,460
Melt the whole stick of butter in

159
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:37,010
a skillet, and cook the onion and

160
00:05:37,010 --> 00:05:39,483
celery slowly, over medium heat,

161
00:05:39,483 --> 00:05:41,492
for ten or twelve minutes,

162
00:05:41,492 --> 00:05:44,351
until they're soft and sweet and just

163
00:05:44,351 --> 00:05:46,051
starting to turn gold.

164
00:05:46,231 --> 00:05:48,433
Then stir in the sage and thyme

165
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:49,853
for one more minute.

166
00:05:50,202 --> 00:05:52,756
Mistake number four is pouring in all

167
00:05:52,756 --> 00:05:53,999
the broth at once.

168
00:05:54,179 --> 00:05:56,995
Every pan of cornbread is different,

169
00:05:56,995 --> 00:05:57,465
honey.

170
00:05:57,645 --> 00:05:59,316
Some soak up more,

171
00:05:59,316 --> 00:06:00,245
some less.

172
00:06:00,425 --> 00:06:03,059
So put your crumbled cornbread and bread

173
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:04,442
in your biggest bowl.

174
00:06:04,622 --> 00:06:07,297
Pour the butter and vegetables over the

175
00:06:07,297 --> 00:06:07,571
top.

176
00:06:07,751 --> 00:06:10,434
Then add three cups of warm broth,

177
00:06:10,434 --> 00:06:13,196
stir gently, and squeeze a handful.

178
00:06:13,376 --> 00:06:15,884
It should feel like a wet sponge.

179
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:18,479
Not dripping, not crumbly.

180
00:06:18,659 --> 00:06:20,888
Add the last cup a little at

181
00:06:20,888 --> 00:06:21,445
a time.

182
00:06:21,796 --> 00:06:23,025
Taste it now,

183
00:06:23,025 --> 00:06:25,104
before the eggs go in.

184
00:06:25,284 --> 00:06:27,148
This is your only chance,

185
00:06:27,148 --> 00:06:27,595
honey.

186
00:06:27,775 --> 00:06:30,195
Add salt and pepper until it tastes

187
00:06:30,195 --> 00:06:31,716
a little too seasoned,

188
00:06:31,716 --> 00:06:34,067
because the baking will mellow it.

189
00:06:34,247 --> 00:06:36,366
Then beat the two eggs and fold

190
00:06:36,366 --> 00:06:38,485
them in gently with your hands.

191
00:06:38,665 --> 00:06:40,894
The eggs are what make it set

192
00:06:40,894 --> 00:06:41,816
into a soft,

193
00:06:41,816 --> 00:06:44,814
sliceable dressing instead of a pile of

194
00:06:44,814 --> 00:06:45,352
crumbs.

195
00:06:45,532 --> 00:06:46,832
Don't stir it hard,

196
00:06:46,832 --> 00:06:48,063
or you'll mash it.

197
00:06:48,414 --> 00:06:50,506
So far, stale cornbread,

198
00:06:50,506 --> 00:06:53,121
a little white bread for glue,

199
00:06:53,121 --> 00:06:57,043
soft vegetables, and broth added slowly until

200
00:06:57,043 --> 00:06:59,048
it feels like a sponge.

201
00:06:59,228 --> 00:07:02,247
Mistake number five is packing it down.

202
00:07:02,427 --> 00:07:05,144
People press their dressing into the pan

203
00:07:05,144 --> 00:07:06,978
like they're laying bricks.

204
00:07:07,158 --> 00:07:09,484
Then it bakes into a dense,

205
00:07:09,484 --> 00:07:10,432
heavy slab.

206
00:07:10,612 --> 00:07:13,077
Grease your pan with butter and spoon

207
00:07:13,077 --> 00:07:14,675
the dressing in loosely.

208
00:07:14,855 --> 00:07:17,410
Leave the top rough and bumpy,

209
00:07:17,410 --> 00:07:17,920
honey.

210
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:20,608
Those bumps are what turn crispy.

211
00:07:20,959 --> 00:07:22,985
Dot the top with a few little

212
00:07:22,985 --> 00:07:24,523
pieces of cold butter.

213
00:07:24,703 --> 00:07:28,091
Bake it uncovered at three hundred seventy-five

214
00:07:28,091 --> 00:07:31,406
degrees for about forty to forty-five minutes,

215
00:07:31,406 --> 00:07:33,857
until the top is deep golden brown

216
00:07:33,857 --> 00:07:36,164
and the edges pull away from the

217
00:07:36,164 --> 00:07:36,452
pan.

218
00:07:36,632 --> 00:07:38,537
If you like it extra crispy,

219
00:07:38,537 --> 00:07:40,851
slide it under the broiler for the

220
00:07:40,851 --> 00:07:42,007
last two minutes.

221
00:07:42,187 --> 00:07:44,670
And let it rest ten minutes before

222
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:45,473
you cut it.

223
00:07:45,653 --> 00:07:46,814
Stay with me,

224
00:07:46,814 --> 00:07:49,403
because now comes the secret.

225
00:07:49,754 --> 00:07:51,663
Here's my mother's secret,

226
00:07:51,663 --> 00:07:52,470
sweetheart.

227
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:53,886
The night before,

228
00:07:53,886 --> 00:07:56,793
when she crumbled her cornbread onto the

229
00:07:56,793 --> 00:07:58,028
sheet pan to dry,

230
00:07:58,028 --> 00:08:00,208
she didn't just leave it bare.

231
00:08:00,388 --> 00:08:02,659
She sprinkled it with a little of

232
00:08:02,659 --> 00:08:04,654
the dried sage and a pinch of

233
00:08:04,654 --> 00:08:04,998
salt.

234
00:08:05,178 --> 00:08:07,483
Then she laid a clean kitchen towel

235
00:08:07,483 --> 00:08:08,866
loosely over the top,

236
00:08:08,866 --> 00:08:10,908
so the dust couldn't get in but

237
00:08:10,908 --> 00:08:12,225
the air still could.

238
00:08:12,405 --> 00:08:14,042
And she let it sit on top

239
00:08:14,042 --> 00:08:15,876
of the warm stove all night.

240
00:08:16,227 --> 00:08:19,010
By morning, the sage had soaked into

241
00:08:19,010 --> 00:08:22,102
every crumb, and the cornbread had dried

242
00:08:22,102 --> 00:08:23,030
just enough.

243
00:08:23,210 --> 00:08:25,431
So the flavor wasn't just in the

244
00:08:25,431 --> 00:08:26,333
broth, honey.

245
00:08:26,513 --> 00:08:28,568
It was in the bread itself.

246
00:08:28,748 --> 00:08:30,431
That's why Mrs.

247
00:08:30,611 --> 00:08:32,864
Hartley could never figure it out.

248
00:08:33,044 --> 00:08:35,981
Hers tasted like sage on the outside.

249
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:38,939
My mother's tasted like sage all the

250
00:08:38,939 --> 00:08:39,865
way through.

251
00:08:40,045 --> 00:08:42,426
And it held the broth perfectly,

252
00:08:42,426 --> 00:08:44,584
without ever turning to mush.

253
00:08:44,935 --> 00:08:46,127
One more thing,

254
00:08:46,127 --> 00:08:47,001
sweetheart.

255
00:08:47,181 --> 00:08:50,288
Don't use plain water instead of broth,

256
00:08:50,288 --> 00:08:52,916
no matter what anybody tells you.

257
00:08:53,096 --> 00:08:55,290
Water makes it taste flat,

258
00:08:55,290 --> 00:08:56,556
like wet bread.

259
00:08:56,736 --> 00:08:57,903
If you have time,

260
00:08:57,903 --> 00:09:00,441
simmer the turkey neck and giblets in

261
00:09:00,441 --> 00:09:02,362
a pot of water with an onion

262
00:09:02,362 --> 00:09:04,627
and a celery stalk while the bird

263
00:09:04,627 --> 00:09:05,107
roasts.

264
00:09:05,287 --> 00:09:08,340
That homemade broth is liquid gold.

265
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:10,545
If you don't have time,

266
00:09:10,545 --> 00:09:13,362
a good boxed broth is just fine.

267
00:09:13,542 --> 00:09:15,225
Mother would forgive you.

268
00:09:15,576 --> 00:09:17,575
You can make it a day ahead,

269
00:09:17,575 --> 00:09:20,647
honey, which saves your sanity on Christmas

270
00:09:20,647 --> 00:09:21,218
morning.

271
00:09:21,398 --> 00:09:23,720
Mix everything except the eggs,

272
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:25,817
cover it, and keep it in the

273
00:09:25,817 --> 00:09:27,540
refrigerator overnight.

274
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:28,925
In the morning,

275
00:09:28,925 --> 00:09:30,291
fold in the eggs,

276
00:09:30,291 --> 00:09:32,058
spoon it into the pan,

277
00:09:32,058 --> 00:09:32,782
and bake.

278
00:09:32,962 --> 00:09:35,204
Give it an extra ten minutes,

279
00:09:35,204 --> 00:09:36,596
because it's cold.

280
00:09:36,776 --> 00:09:38,556
Leftovers keep four days,

281
00:09:38,556 --> 00:09:40,834
and a square fried in butter the

282
00:09:40,834 --> 00:09:43,113
next morning with a fried egg on

283
00:09:43,113 --> 00:09:45,605
top is my favorite breakfast of the

284
00:09:45,605 --> 00:09:46,389
whole year.

285
00:09:46,739 --> 00:09:48,351
When my mother passed,

286
00:09:48,351 --> 00:09:50,109
in nineteen eighty-nine,

287
00:09:50,109 --> 00:09:52,381
Susan was the one who asked for

288
00:09:52,381 --> 00:09:53,554
her recipe card.

289
00:09:53,734 --> 00:09:54,761
It just says,

290
00:09:54,761 --> 00:09:58,240
cornbread dressing, in Mother's handwriting,

291
00:09:58,240 --> 00:09:59,901
with the ingredients,

292
00:09:59,901 --> 00:10:01,324
and at the bottom,

293
00:10:01,324 --> 00:10:02,905
in pencil, one word.

294
00:10:03,085 --> 00:10:03,794
Towel.

295
00:10:03,974 --> 00:10:07,004
Nobody knew what it meant except me.

296
00:10:07,184 --> 00:10:08,937
Now Susan knows.

297
00:10:09,117 --> 00:10:10,832
And so do you,

298
00:10:10,832 --> 00:10:11,567
honey.

299
00:10:11,917 --> 00:10:13,606
Make it this Christmas,

300
00:10:13,606 --> 00:10:16,619
sweetheart, and watch it disappear first.

301
00:10:16,799 --> 00:10:18,466
Tell me in the comments,

302
00:10:18,466 --> 00:10:21,384
does your family say stuffing or dressing?

303
00:10:21,564 --> 00:10:24,039
I want to settle this once and

304
00:10:24,039 --> 00:10:24,699
for all.

305
00:10:24,879 --> 00:10:27,179
There are more of my Christmas recipes

306
00:10:27,179 --> 00:10:29,297
in the playlist right there on your

307
00:10:29,297 --> 00:10:29,721
screen.

308
00:10:29,901 --> 00:10:31,480
Good night, honey.

309
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,146
Merry Christmas.
